Fillets: where the prolonged edge at a rounding's end crosses the seam of
the opposite face, its two pieces get their p-curves on the face at the
end from that face.

At the end of a rounding, PerformOneCorner prolongs the edge between the
face at the end (Fv) and the opposite face (Fop) from the vertex to the
contact's end: the intersection curve of the two faces, with a p-curve on
each. Where that curve crosses the seam of the opposite face (a cylinder
whose seam lies between the edge and the contact), the curve is cut at
the seam in two pieces. The pieces' p-curves on the face at the end were
computed by projecting them onto the opposite face, Fop, and stored on
Fv: on Fv they lay anywhere (edges with tolerances of several mm), and the
face at the end was unorientable. The pieces now take the intersection's
own p-curve on Fv, trimmed at the seam crossing, as the opposite face's
pieces are computed on that face.
